          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is the symptoms and abnormal findings chapter for, and when should a code from it be used instead of a disease code?",
          "answer": "The \"Symptoms and Abnormal Findings\" chapter in the ICD-10 coding system (codes R00-R99) is designed for coding various symptoms, signs, and abnormal findings when no definitive diagnosis is yet established. This chapter includes codes for a wide range of conditions, such as general symptoms (like fever and fatigue), abnormal laboratory results, and specific complaints related to various body systems.\n\nYou should use a code from this chapter instead of disease codes when a patient presents with symptoms or abnormal findings but does not have a confirmed diagnosis. For example, chest pain or abnormal imaging results should initially be coded as symptoms until a specific diagnosis is made. Similarly, abnormal laboratory findings without an associated diagnosis also fall under this chapter.\n\nThis approach helps ensure accurate documentation and facilitates proper clinical management while further investigations are ongoing. More details can be found in the document titled \"ICD-10-CM Chapter 18: symptoms, signs and abnormal findings\" (coding-icd10-chapter-18-symptoms).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
